index 0000000..2bb1782
--- /dev/null
+++ b/specs/hal-engine-tool-input-validation/spec.md
@@ -0,0 +1,29 @@
+# Tool Input Validation
+
+| Field  | Value                |
+| ------ | -------------------- |
+| Issue  | re-cinq/HALEngine#37 |
+| Status | In Progress          |
+
+Every tool registered in `src/orchestration/tools/registry.ts` declares a JSON Schema `inputSchema` that is forwarded to the configured AI provider, and `ToolRegistry.execute` validates the model-supplied `input` against that schema before calling the tool's executor. When validation fails, `execute` returns a rejection `ToolResponse` describing what was wrong rather than throwing, so the orchestrator feeds the failure back to the model as a `tool_result` within the existing tool-round budget.
+
+## Validation at execute time
+
+- When `input` omits a property listed in `required`, `ToolRegistry.execute` returns a `ToolResponse` whose `result` names the missing property as required, including its full path when nested, and never invokes the executor ([validated by: rejects input missing a required property with a ToolResponse and never calls the executor](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L16), [names the full path of a nested required property](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.test.ts#L169)).
+- When `input` supplies a property whose value does not match its declared JSON Schema type, `ToolRegistry.execute` returns a `ToolResponse` naming the failing field path and expected type without invoking the executor, and neither `result` nor the emitted log record contains the offending property value. Escaped JSON Pointer property names and legacy array indexes are decoded into readable field paths ([validated by: rejects a wrong-typed property naming the field and expected type without leaking the offending value to the result or logs](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L46), [reports escaped JSON pointer property names as readable field paths](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.test.ts#L152), [reports a nested escaped property name as a full field path](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.test.ts#L128), [reports a legacy array index in the full failing field path](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.test.ts#L108)).
+- When `input` satisfies `required`, declared property types and `enum`, `ToolRegistry.execute` invokes the executor once and passes the input object through unchanged — including undeclared extra properties — without injecting `additionalProperties: false`, coercing types or applying defaults ([validated by: passes valid input with an extra property to the executor unchanged once](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.test.ts#L191)).
+- When `ToolRegistry.execute` is called with a tool name that has not been registered, it throws `Unknown tool: <name>` because an unregistered tool is a programming or configuration error rather than a recoverable input mistake.
+
+## Schema compilation and caching
+
+- A static `ToolDefinition` has its `inputSchema` compiled once at `register` time, so a schema that is not valid JSON Schema throws synchronously from `register` with an error message naming the offending tool ([validated by: throws at register time naming the tool when a static inputSchema cannot be compiled](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L94), [rejects an invalid static schema at registration with the tool name](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.test.ts#L12)).
+- A static tool's `inputSchema` is compiled at `register` time and reused on every `execute` call without re-reading or re-compiling `definition.inputSchema` ([validated by: keeps using the schema compiled at registration](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.test.ts#L65)).
+- A function-valued `ToolDefinitionSource` resolves its definition on each `execute` call and validates against the `inputSchema` returned for that call, caching compiled validators by schema object reference (`WeakMap`) so distinct returned schemas each enforce their own rules ([validated by: validates each resolved schema and compiles each schema object once](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.test.ts#L30)).
+
+## Orchestrator tool-loop recovery
+
+- Because a schema rejection returns a `ToolResponse` instead of throwing, `src/orchestration/chatOrchestrator.ts` sends the rejection `result` back to the provider as a `tool_result` message, allowing the model to issue a corrected tool call on the next round and complete the turn within `maxToolRounds` ([validated by: returns a schema rejection as a tool_result in the orchestrator loop so the model can correct the call on the next round](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L112)).
+
+## Enum rejection
+
+- When an input value violates a declared `enum`, `ToolRegistry.execute` returns a rejection naming the field and enum constraint without invoking the executor or echoing the rejected value ([validated by: rejects an enum violation with the field and constraint but no input value](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.test.ts#L87)).
